WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.282 --> 00:00:01.215
(suspenseful music)

00:00:01.215 --> 00:00:03.090
- [Narrator] It is August 5, 2010

00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:06.210
at the San Jose Gold and
Copper Mine in Chile,

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:08.910
and more than 2,000
feet below the surface,

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:11.880
a couple of miners notice
a faint rumbling sound

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:13.225
echoing through the rocks.

00:00:13.225 --> 00:00:15.510
(ground rumbling)

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:18.090
The miners have heard
rumblings like this before

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:21.570
as the rock adjusts to new excavations,

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:25.961
but today these rumblings are
much more frequent than usual.

00:00:25.961 --> 00:00:26.794
(clock ticking)

00:00:26.794 --> 00:00:28.200
The time is 2:00 PM,

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:30.630
and the workers are trying
to wrap up as much work

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:33.330
as possible before they will
be picked up by a work truck,

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:36.507
which will drive them all the
way to the surface for lunch.

00:00:36.507 --> 00:00:37.470
(suspenseful music fades out)

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:40.770
But then, suddenly, they
hear something much louder

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:42.306
than the previous rumblings.

00:00:42.306 --> 00:00:45.069
(ground rumbling loudly)

00:00:45.069 --> 00:00:46.410
(intense music)

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:49.140
The blast sends those
working on the higher levels

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:50.700
of the mine to the ground

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:53.463
and causes the tunnels
and shafts to collapse.

00:00:53.463 --> 00:00:54.870
(rocks clacking)

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:59.130
Large rocks tumble down,
sweeping across the workers,

00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:02.526
and all they can do is hang
onto them and ride out.

00:01:02.526 --> 00:01:03.960
(rocks clacking)

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:07.020
The workers have no way of knowing yet,

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:09.930
but the San Jose mine has just collapsed,

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:12.209
trapping them beneath the surface.

00:01:12.209 --> 00:01:15.900
(intense music continues)

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:18.570
The mine is located in the Atacama Desert,

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:21.690
one of the driest areas in the world.

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:24.780
Conditions here are so extreme
that the site is often used

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:27.450
for simulations of Mars expeditions,

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:29.310
and yet, the Atacama Desert

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:32.880
is an incredibly valuable asset to Chile

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:35.940
and that is because of what lies beneath:

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:40.940
abundant mineral resources
from copper to silver to gold.

00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:43.710
When looking at the
mine from above ground,

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:45.780
its true scale is hidden.

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:49.980
To go in, there's an unassuming
five by five meter entrance,

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:53.280
and it leads to a tunnel,
known as the ramp,

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:55.080
that spirals downwards

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:58.980
with several branching paths
leading off into the mountain,

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:01.170
the workers orient themselves underground

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:05.250
based on a level system using
sea level for reference.

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:08.310
The entrance is at level 720,

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:11.346
which is 720 meters above sea.

00:02:11.346 --> 00:02:12.900
(intense music continues)

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:14.100
When the mine collapsed,

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:17.973
some of the workers were
as deep as level 40.

00:02:19.097 --> 00:02:20.130
(truck engine rumbling)

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:22.110
In the immediate
aftermath of the collapse,

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:25.980
some of the workers tried to
make a break for the surface.

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:29.490
They get into a truck
and drive up the ramp.

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:31.830
However, they quickly get stuck,

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:33.720
as the road is so littered with debris

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:36.750
that they need to continue on foot.

00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:39.120
They reach level 190

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:41.733
where they find the cause of the collapse:

00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:44.880
a giant slab of diorite

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:48.000
estimated as tall as a 45-story building

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:51.030
and weighing 770,000 tons

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:52.830
has broken off from the mountain

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:54.900
and crashed down the mine

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:57.063
completely blocking the ramp.

00:02:58.050 --> 00:02:59.550
The workers are trapped,

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:03.690
and their only option now
is to retreat back down.

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:05.490
Located at level 90,

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:09.510
there is a small enclosed
space known as The Refuge.

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:12.270
It is designated as an
emergency shelter area

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:14.790
for situations like this.

00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:17.430
Fresh air is pumped in from the surface

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:19.290
and emergency food and medicine

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:21.060
is locked in metal cabinets

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:23.385
that are to be opened
should the need arise.

00:03:23.385 --> 00:03:27.060
(intense music continues)

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:29.760
As the workers descend
back down into the mine,

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:32.190
the word spreads of the collapse

00:03:32.190 --> 00:03:35.310
until the news eventually
reaches the shift supervisor,

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:39.480
Luis Urzua, who is working at level 90.

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:42.360
He orders the workers to
take shelter in The Refuge

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:44.250
and heads down further into the mine

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:47.040
to evacuate those working below.

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:49.080
Because of the distance from the collapse

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:51.270
and ear protection worn by the men,

00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:54.270
those down below didn't
realize what was happening

00:03:54.270 --> 00:03:58.080
until Urzua orders them to The Refuge.

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:00.510
Once everyone has gathered together,

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:02.730
Urzua then conducts a roll call

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:05.463
and counts 33 man trapped.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:08.790
With the ramp completely closed,

00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:11.070
some of the workers realized
that they might be able

00:04:11.070 --> 00:04:13.620
to escape through the ventilation shafts

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:17.040
that lead to various parts of the mine.

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:19.350
According to standard safety regulations,

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:21.126
there should be ladders that can be used

00:04:21.126 --> 00:04:23.223
to climb through those shafts.

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:27.420
To investigate this, a
small group breaks off,

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:31.529
but when they get there, they
discover a heartbreaking site.

00:04:31.529 --> 00:04:32.940
(suspenseful music)

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:35.640
There's no ladder in the shaft.

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:39.003
The owners of the mine never
finished installing them.

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:41.310
Back at the surface,

00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:45.480
the dust cloud has begun
pouring out of the entrance.

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:47.220
As the supervisors realize

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:49.800
that something must have
gone terribly wrong,

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:52.200
one man, who at the time of the collapse

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:54.030
was driving up the ramp,

00:04:54.030 --> 00:04:58.023
is able to make it out through
the blinding ash of dust.

00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:00.630
With the supervisors present,

00:05:00.630 --> 00:05:03.930
one would expect immediate
help would be organized.

00:05:03.930 --> 00:05:07.170
However, they decide that
there is nothing they can do

00:05:07.170 --> 00:05:09.301
until the dust fully settles.

00:05:09.301 --> 00:05:11.310
(suspenseful music continues)

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:14.940
Hours pass until they begin
their descent down the ramp

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:17.970
where they encountered the blockage.

00:05:17.970 --> 00:05:20.460
Standing face to face with the enormity

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:21.900
of the diorite block

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:24.180
completely obstructing the ramp,

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:28.560
the supervisors realize that
there is no coming through,

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:32.580
and still, precious time
is lost in the next steps.

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:34.770
Despite having a phone line at the mine

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:36.960
for emergencies such as this,

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:39.780
the mine's owners decide
to instead head back

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:41.550
towards the closest town

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:45.060
in order to get cell
service to call for help.

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:48.480
Finally, after more than
five hours since the collapse

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:51.090
authorities are called.

00:05:51.090 --> 00:05:54.090
The news gets passed from
the local fire department

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:56.490
and onward until it eventually reaches

00:05:56.490 --> 00:06:00.084
the Disaster Office of Chile's
Ministry of the Interior.

00:06:00.084 --> 00:06:02.820
(intense music)

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:05.880
The government deployed
special forces in climbing gear

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:09.630
with the hope that they could
free the trapped miners.

00:06:09.630 --> 00:06:11.160
The idea is the same one

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:13.650
that the miners had in
the immediate aftermath:

00:06:13.650 --> 00:06:17.163
using the ventilation
shafts as an escape route.

00:06:18.630 --> 00:06:22.200
As these forces climb down
the shafts, secured to ropes,

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:26.040
they are alarmed to see cracks
in the walls of the shaft,

00:06:26.040 --> 00:06:29.610
a result of the diorite block
pushing against the walls

00:06:29.610 --> 00:06:31.173
and squeezing the shaft.

00:06:31.173 --> 00:06:32.520
(intense music continues)

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:35.130
Despite the danger of another collapse,

00:06:35.130 --> 00:06:36.780
the rescuers continue on

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:39.993
determined to reach any man trapped below.

00:06:41.190 --> 00:06:43.950
They're able to make it to level 268

00:06:43.950 --> 00:06:46.920
before they face a pile of rock and debris

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:49.260
that looks as if it
could fall on top of them

00:06:49.260 --> 00:06:50.910
at any moment.

00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:53.700
These shafts are way too unstable

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:56.010
and as they begin to retreat back,

00:06:56.010 --> 00:06:59.640
suddenly the rocks begin
to fall around them

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:03.210
proving that their worries are justified.

00:07:03.210 --> 00:07:06.480
The rescuers manage to make it
out of the ventilation shafts

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:07.923
and back to safety,

00:07:08.820 --> 00:07:10.470
but now they need to report

00:07:10.470 --> 00:07:12.960
to those waiting anxiously on the surface

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:16.380
that a rescue like this
will be impossible.

00:07:16.380 --> 00:07:19.353
The only option left will be to drill.

00:07:21.093 --> 00:07:22.860
(suspenseful music)

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:26.910
2,300 feet beneath the rescuers
delivering the bad news

00:07:26.910 --> 00:07:29.730
the workers who returned
from their own failed attempt

00:07:29.730 --> 00:07:31.770
at navigating the ventilation shafts

00:07:31.770 --> 00:07:34.290
must also return to The Refuge

00:07:34.290 --> 00:07:37.230
with bad news of their own.

00:07:37.230 --> 00:07:38.910
As experienced miners,

00:07:38.910 --> 00:07:43.830
many realized that this is
going to take days if not weeks.

00:07:43.830 --> 00:07:45.450
The enormity of what has happened

00:07:45.450 --> 00:07:47.250
hits some harder than others,

00:07:47.250 --> 00:07:51.390
and it particularly affects
Shift Supervisor Urzua.

00:07:51.390 --> 00:07:53.310
Urzua makes an announcement

00:07:53.310 --> 00:07:55.110
that, beginning with this moment,

00:07:55.110 --> 00:07:56.940
he's no longer the boss,

00:07:56.940 --> 00:08:00.690
and that they will need to
treat each other as equals.

00:08:00.690 --> 00:08:02.640
Knowing they're going
to be trapped for long,

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:04.380
the miners next take inventory

00:08:04.380 --> 00:08:06.330
of the supplies in The Refuge,

00:08:06.330 --> 00:08:10.080
and the workers are soon
met with another heartbreak.

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:11.910
What was supposed to be enough food

00:08:11.910 --> 00:08:15.000
to feed an entire shift
of workers for two days

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:17.580
turned out to be one can of salmon,

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:21.897
one can of peaches, one can
of peas, 18 cans of tuna,

00:08:21.897 --> 00:08:24.840
93 packages of cookies,

00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:28.800
24 liters of condensed
milk, eight of them expired,

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:33.800
10 bottles of water, and
some medicine, also expired.

00:08:33.870 --> 00:08:37.050
All of this will need to
be shared among 33 men

00:08:37.050 --> 00:08:40.153
and has to last potentially for weeks.

00:08:40.153 --> 00:08:42.300
(somber music)

00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:44.400
Looking at the meager food reserves,

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:45.900
the men come up with a plan

00:08:45.900 --> 00:08:48.810
to make it last as long as possible.

00:08:48.810 --> 00:08:51.270
Every miner will get one meal a day

00:08:51.270 --> 00:08:54.930
consisting of one cookie
and one spoonful of tuna

00:08:54.930 --> 00:08:57.120
mixed with water that has been heated

00:08:57.120 --> 00:08:59.220
to turn it into a broth.

00:08:59.220 --> 00:09:02.430
It is a meal that has
less than 300 calories,

00:09:02.430 --> 00:09:04.890
but it is all they can do.

00:09:04.890 --> 00:09:08.190
Drinking water, fortunately,
is less of an issue.

00:09:08.190 --> 00:09:10.260
Even though they only have 10 bottles

00:09:10.260 --> 00:09:11.910
of clean, fresh water,

00:09:11.910 --> 00:09:13.860
there are large tanks nearby

00:09:13.860 --> 00:09:17.070
filled with thousands of
liters of industrial water

00:09:17.070 --> 00:09:18.660
that is used to keep the engines

00:09:18.660 --> 00:09:20.820
of their machines cool.

00:09:20.820 --> 00:09:22.410
This water is drinkable,

00:09:22.410 --> 00:09:24.780
even though it isn't exactly desirable,

00:09:24.780 --> 00:09:27.033
as it is contaminated with oil.

00:09:27.990 --> 00:09:31.620
With a somewhat stable plan
for food and water set,

00:09:31.620 --> 00:09:35.160
a new constant threat
begins to materialize:

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:36.842
mental collapse.

00:09:36.842 --> 00:09:37.950
(somber music)

00:09:37.950 --> 00:09:40.440
Would anyone even be searching for them

00:09:40.440 --> 00:09:42.390
or would they just be presumed dead

00:09:42.390 --> 00:09:45.390
and the mine be sealed off?

00:09:45.390 --> 00:09:47.100
To keep these shadows at bay,

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:48.870
one of the workers, an electrician,

00:09:48.870 --> 00:09:52.590
figures out a way to keep
their flashlights charged.

00:09:52.590 --> 00:09:55.560
He does so by wiring them
up to the large batteries

00:09:55.560 --> 00:09:56.823
of the work vehicles,

00:09:57.720 --> 00:10:00.780
and the miners even decide
to use the artificial light

00:10:00.780 --> 00:10:03.180
to simulate night and day,

00:10:03.180 --> 00:10:04.440
keeping track of time

00:10:04.440 --> 00:10:09.060
and maintaining a comforting
sleep rhythm for themselves.

00:10:09.060 --> 00:10:10.590
But the light isn't enough

00:10:10.590 --> 00:10:13.170
to fend off the shadows of the mind,

00:10:13.170 --> 00:10:16.800
and so the pastor decides to step in.

00:10:16.800 --> 00:10:20.610
Jose Henriquez isn't
actually a pastor by trade,

00:10:20.610 --> 00:10:23.910
but he is a devout evangelical man.

00:10:23.910 --> 00:10:26.430
The workers soon call him Pastor,

00:10:26.430 --> 00:10:28.380
a nickname that he earns

00:10:28.380 --> 00:10:31.020
because he leads the men in prayer.

00:10:31.020 --> 00:10:35.190
These prayer sessions become
a fixture in the men's cycle.

00:10:35.190 --> 00:10:37.290
Have a restless sleep throughout the night

00:10:37.290 --> 00:10:38.580
plagued by nightmares,

00:10:38.580 --> 00:10:40.710
wake up to the artificial sun

00:10:40.710 --> 00:10:42.150
that is their flashlights,

00:10:42.150 --> 00:10:45.930
join together for a sermon
delivered by Pastor Henriquez,

00:10:45.930 --> 00:10:48.570
and then eat a single meal of one cookie

00:10:48.570 --> 00:10:51.330
and broth made from oily water,

00:10:51.330 --> 00:10:54.210
and most of all, simply wait,

00:10:54.210 --> 00:10:55.980
never knowing if anyone

00:10:55.980 --> 00:10:58.173
is even bothering to search for them.

00:10:59.146 --> 00:11:01.110
(intense music)

00:11:01.110 --> 00:11:04.200
Though completely separated
from the society above,

00:11:04.200 --> 00:11:08.520
the miners created their own
functioning society underground

00:11:08.520 --> 00:11:10.500
from pastor, to doctor,

00:11:10.500 --> 00:11:13.440
and, although he had
formally stepped down,

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:17.580
Urzua still was seen by
many as some form of leader.

00:11:17.580 --> 00:11:21.150
You could think of it
as a city below ground

00:11:21.150 --> 00:11:23.730
and just like there was a city below,

00:11:23.730 --> 00:11:28.110
above ground, a new city
also started forming.

00:11:28.110 --> 00:11:30.480
Many of the miner's
families were frustrated

00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:32.250
by the lack of communication,

00:11:32.250 --> 00:11:34.035
and news reports offered confusing

00:11:34.035 --> 00:11:36.480
and conflicting information.

00:11:36.480 --> 00:11:39.030
Therefore, many wives, and parents,

00:11:39.030 --> 00:11:42.512
and children decided to
head to the mine directly.

00:11:42.512 --> 00:11:44.430
(intense music)

00:11:44.430 --> 00:11:47.160
One of the miner's sisters, Maria Segovia,

00:11:47.160 --> 00:11:49.200
immediately begins to take charge

00:11:49.200 --> 00:11:51.120
on behalf of other family members

00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:53.850
in demanding to know
what is going to be done

00:11:53.850 --> 00:11:55.443
to rescue the miners.

00:11:56.610 --> 00:12:00.330
She declares that the families
will camp outside of the mine

00:12:00.330 --> 00:12:03.330
until their loved ones
are returned to them,

00:12:03.330 --> 00:12:04.848
and others follow her.

00:12:04.848 --> 00:12:06.090
(intense music continues)

00:12:06.090 --> 00:12:07.560
Tents are brought out,

00:12:07.560 --> 00:12:08.610
and the families begin

00:12:08.610 --> 00:12:10.983
to sleep every night at the site.

00:12:12.030 --> 00:12:15.240
And every day, Segovia once again, demands

00:12:15.240 --> 00:12:17.430
to know what is happening

00:12:17.430 --> 00:12:21.000
until she and the other
family members are pushed back

00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:24.210
and a perimeter is set
up around the rescue site

00:12:24.210 --> 00:12:27.303
with only authorized
personal allowed to enter.

00:12:28.527 --> 00:12:30.900
(spoon clacking)

00:12:30.900 --> 00:12:32.010
Not to be deterred,

00:12:32.010 --> 00:12:34.950
Segovia leads efforts
to get more information

00:12:34.950 --> 00:12:36.780
by banging pots and pans

00:12:36.780 --> 00:12:38.607
at the edge of the perimeter...

00:12:38.607 --> 00:12:39.690
(intense music)

00:12:39.690 --> 00:12:41.850
(clacking continues)

00:12:41.850 --> 00:12:46.049
Until an official comes over
to let them know the progress.

00:12:46.049 --> 00:12:48.630
(intense music continues)

00:12:48.630 --> 00:12:50.280
And with every passing day,

00:12:50.280 --> 00:12:54.600
the tents continue to grow
into essentially a new city,

00:12:54.600 --> 00:12:57.810
and its residents decide
to give it a name:

00:12:57.810 --> 00:12:58.643
Camp Hope.

00:12:58.643 --> 00:13:00.870
(intense music continues)

00:13:00.870 --> 00:13:04.950
Meanwhile, Segovia also
earns herself a nickname:

00:13:04.950 --> 00:13:07.983
La Alcaldesa, The Mayor of Camp Hope.

00:13:09.189 --> 00:13:10.564
(intense music fades out)

00:13:10.564 --> 00:13:12.600
(intense music)

00:13:12.600 --> 00:13:15.780
Without knowing about the
formation of Camp Hope above them,

00:13:15.780 --> 00:13:18.600
by the time the fourth day
underground comes about,

00:13:18.600 --> 00:13:21.630
the workers are increasingly worried,

00:13:21.630 --> 00:13:26.400
that is until someone hears a
faint sound in the distance.

00:13:26.400 --> 00:13:29.220
Everyone quiets, and they listen together,

00:13:29.220 --> 00:13:31.271
scarcely able to believe it.

00:13:31.271 --> 00:13:32.520
(drill rattling)

00:13:32.520 --> 00:13:35.984
It is the sound of a drill
traveling through the mountain.

00:13:35.984 --> 00:13:37.680
(drill rattling)

00:13:37.680 --> 00:13:40.500
Up above, a rotary drill
has started its work

00:13:40.500 --> 00:13:44.100
creating a borehole down to
an area close to The Refuge

00:13:44.100 --> 00:13:47.970
where rescuers correctly
believe the workers to be.

00:13:47.970 --> 00:13:50.130
But there are some issues.

00:13:50.130 --> 00:13:51.360
The simplest method would be

00:13:51.360 --> 00:13:53.730
to drill straight down to their target.

00:13:53.730 --> 00:13:56.790
However, this would mean setting
up the massive drilling rig

00:13:56.790 --> 00:13:59.100
right above the cavity left behind

00:13:59.100 --> 00:14:00.990
by the giant diorite block

00:14:00.990 --> 00:14:02.700
that has broken off,

00:14:02.700 --> 00:14:04.470
and, because of that cavity,

00:14:04.470 --> 00:14:07.410
the ground is too unstable here.

00:14:07.410 --> 00:14:09.330
Therefore, the drill has to be set up

00:14:09.330 --> 00:14:11.100
at a different location,

00:14:11.100 --> 00:14:15.393
and the hole will need to
be dug at a 78 degree angle.

00:14:16.410 --> 00:14:20.670
Their target is a small area 32 feet wide,

00:14:20.670 --> 00:14:24.030
and they will need to travel
through over 2,000 feet

00:14:24.030 --> 00:14:25.863
of diorite to reach it.

00:14:26.730 --> 00:14:29.880
If the drill deviates
just by a small amount,

00:14:29.880 --> 00:14:31.980
by the time they reached the target,

00:14:31.980 --> 00:14:34.860
they could be completely off.

00:14:34.860 --> 00:14:36.960
It is at this point during the rescue

00:14:36.960 --> 00:14:40.320
that yet another person
joins the operation.

00:14:40.320 --> 00:14:43.560
Andre Sougarret works as an
administrator and engineer

00:14:43.560 --> 00:14:46.140
for the El Teniente Mine in Chile.

00:14:46.140 --> 00:14:49.830
This mine is significantly
larger than the San Jose mine,

00:14:49.830 --> 00:14:52.410
and rescues are much more common there,

00:14:52.410 --> 00:14:55.830
which makes Sougarret an
expert in these situations.

00:14:55.830 --> 00:14:57.600
(intense music)

00:14:57.600 --> 00:15:00.750
Once he arrives, Sougarret
immediately gets to work,

00:15:00.750 --> 00:15:04.380
taking over and scaling
up the rescue operation.

00:15:04.380 --> 00:15:06.930
He deploys eight additional drills,

00:15:06.930 --> 00:15:09.180
all of them to run concurrently,

00:15:09.180 --> 00:15:10.920
each making its own borehole

00:15:10.920 --> 00:15:13.560
with the same target near The Refuge.

00:15:13.560 --> 00:15:15.480
He knows they need to work fast,

00:15:15.480 --> 00:15:17.430
and the more opportunities they have

00:15:17.430 --> 00:15:19.244
to hit the target, the better.

00:15:19.244 --> 00:15:20.340
(intense music continues)

00:15:20.340 --> 00:15:21.630
While the drills continue,

00:15:21.630 --> 00:15:25.200
Sougarret decides to
simultaneously revisit the idea

00:15:25.200 --> 00:15:28.020
of using the ventilation shafts.

00:15:28.020 --> 00:15:30.450
He sends climbers back into the mine

00:15:30.450 --> 00:15:32.940
with the goal to clear these shafts,

00:15:32.940 --> 00:15:35.760
and as the rescuers get
to work on this plan,

00:15:35.760 --> 00:15:39.120
they soon make a scary observation.

00:15:39.120 --> 00:15:41.010
They realize that the diorite block

00:15:41.010 --> 00:15:42.630
is actually still moving,

00:15:42.630 --> 00:15:46.140
slowly sinking lower
and lower into the mine.

00:15:46.140 --> 00:15:48.720
It could completely
collapse at any moment,

00:15:48.720 --> 00:15:50.973
and a difficult decision is made.

00:15:51.870 --> 00:15:54.840
The entrance of the San
Jose mine needs to be closed

00:15:54.840 --> 00:15:56.430
and sealed off permanently,

00:15:56.430 --> 00:15:58.590
and they will need to
put all of their hopes

00:15:58.590 --> 00:16:00.034
into the drills.

00:16:00.034 --> 00:16:01.290
(suspenseful music)

00:16:01.290 --> 00:16:04.320
And as if closing the mine
wasn't bad news enough,

00:16:04.320 --> 00:16:07.530
the drilling is not
going according to plan.

00:16:07.530 --> 00:16:09.180
After three days of digging,

00:16:09.180 --> 00:16:12.570
one of the drills is bending
in the wrong direction.

00:16:12.570 --> 00:16:13.980
With no way to fix it,

00:16:13.980 --> 00:16:16.110
this hole will need to be given up on

00:16:16.110 --> 00:16:18.750
and restarted at a different location.

00:16:18.750 --> 00:16:21.330
And it's seeming more and
more likely to rescuers

00:16:21.330 --> 00:16:22.620
that the workers down below

00:16:22.620 --> 00:16:25.740
would surely have perished by this point.

00:16:25.740 --> 00:16:27.390
The drills continue on

00:16:27.390 --> 00:16:30.510
with few believing they
will reach anything

00:16:30.510 --> 00:16:34.530
when suddenly the unthinkable happens.

00:16:34.530 --> 00:16:38.340
The drill has broken into
a cavern in the mine,

00:16:38.340 --> 00:16:40.350
the loud drilling is stopped,

00:16:40.350 --> 00:16:41.400
and the rescuers think

00:16:41.400 --> 00:16:44.181
that they can hear a
tapping sound on the drill.

00:16:44.181 --> 00:16:45.660
(metal clanking)

00:16:45.660 --> 00:16:47.250
Scarcely able to believe it,

00:16:47.250 --> 00:16:48.750
they quickly bring up the drill

00:16:48.750 --> 00:16:51.120
in order to lower a camera instead.

00:16:51.120 --> 00:16:52.200
And once that is lowered,

00:16:52.200 --> 00:16:54.150
they turn the camera around the cavern,

00:16:54.150 --> 00:16:55.833
capturing the walls,

00:16:56.760 --> 00:17:00.543
but unfortunately they
see nothing but emptiness.

00:17:01.648 --> 00:17:02.481
(somber music)

00:17:02.481 --> 00:17:03.630
With sunken hearts,

00:17:03.630 --> 00:17:05.910
they raise their camera back up.

00:17:05.910 --> 00:17:08.580
The tapping sound could
have been anything,

00:17:08.580 --> 00:17:10.203
and they go back to drilling.

00:17:11.160 --> 00:17:15.750
Down below, in The Refuge, the
workers are in a rough shape.

00:17:15.750 --> 00:17:18.480
It has been over two weeks
since they've been trapped,

00:17:18.480 --> 00:17:20.040
and, in order to survive longer,

00:17:20.040 --> 00:17:23.280
they now only eat every second day.

00:17:23.280 --> 00:17:25.530
They can see the weight
loss on each other,

00:17:25.530 --> 00:17:27.750
and, with now noticeably less energy,

00:17:27.750 --> 00:17:30.300
it is harder to keep moving.

00:17:30.300 --> 00:17:32.160
Some of the workers even venture down

00:17:32.160 --> 00:17:33.900
lower into the depths of the mine

00:17:33.900 --> 00:17:36.810
to see if the drill will
break through somewhere there,

00:17:36.810 --> 00:17:39.150
but they realize it is too dangerous

00:17:39.150 --> 00:17:41.400
and head to back up.

00:17:41.400 --> 00:17:44.160
Back on the surface,
after so many failures,

00:17:44.160 --> 00:17:47.490
Sougarret has decided
on a risky new approach.

00:17:47.490 --> 00:17:50.940
Instead of digging as fast as
they can to reach the workers,

00:17:50.940 --> 00:17:53.370
they will now go painstakingly slow,

00:17:53.370 --> 00:17:56.520
sacrificing speed for better accuracy.

00:17:56.520 --> 00:17:59.010
It will take more days
to reach their target,

00:17:59.010 --> 00:18:02.580
but if they keep missing,
it'll take even longer anyway.

00:18:02.580 --> 00:18:06.033
The drills grind to half
of their original speed.

00:18:07.260 --> 00:18:10.200
Days pass with this new slower approach,

00:18:10.200 --> 00:18:12.030
and by August 22nd,

00:18:12.030 --> 00:18:15.570
it has now been 17 days
since the collapse.

00:18:15.570 --> 00:18:18.150
Many have privately given up hope,

00:18:18.150 --> 00:18:20.250
but none dare to voice their thoughts

00:18:20.250 --> 00:18:21.870
that it seems unlikely

00:18:21.870 --> 00:18:25.020
they will find anyone alive at this point.

00:18:25.020 --> 00:18:27.780
And then it happens again.

00:18:27.780 --> 00:18:28.920
One of the drills reaches

00:18:28.920 --> 00:18:31.470
an open cavern near the target area,

00:18:31.470 --> 00:18:33.450
and, once again, the rescuers think

00:18:33.450 --> 00:18:35.820
they hear tapping on the drill.

00:18:35.820 --> 00:18:38.670
Not wanting to have a repeat of last time,

00:18:38.670 --> 00:18:42.240
they brace themselves to
avoid getting their hopes up.

00:18:42.240 --> 00:18:45.690
Once again, they send
the camera back down,

00:18:45.690 --> 00:18:48.903
and this time something peculiar happens.

00:18:49.950 --> 00:18:51.690
Once the drill reaches the surface,

00:18:51.690 --> 00:18:53.580
someone points out that there is a small,

00:18:53.580 --> 00:18:56.790
palm-sized red smudge on the drill.

00:18:56.790 --> 00:18:58.102
Was that there earlier?

00:18:58.102 --> 00:18:59.580
(suspenseful music)

00:18:59.580 --> 00:19:01.410
Before they can investigate further,

00:19:01.410 --> 00:19:02.400
someone else notices

00:19:02.400 --> 00:19:05.471
that there is something
attached to the drill bit.

00:19:05.471 --> 00:19:07.920
Tied to it, with rubber tubing,

00:19:07.920 --> 00:19:10.170
there are pieces of paper.

00:19:10.170 --> 00:19:11.760
There's no mistaking this.

00:19:11.760 --> 00:19:15.000
Someone down below must
have attached this.

00:19:15.000 --> 00:19:16.770
Carefully unwrapping the tubing,

00:19:16.770 --> 00:19:20.280
rescuers begin to read
the three pieces of paper.

00:19:20.280 --> 00:19:22.320
The first is a note that points out

00:19:22.320 --> 00:19:25.710
where exactly the drill
has broken through.

00:19:25.710 --> 00:19:29.100
The second is a personal
letter to a family member.

00:19:29.100 --> 00:19:33.720
And the third is the one that
completely changes everything.

00:19:33.720 --> 00:19:37.952
It says, "We are well
in The Refuge, the 33."

00:19:37.952 --> 00:19:39.720
(people cheering)

00:19:39.720 --> 00:19:42.270
Cheers ring out among the rescuers,

00:19:42.270 --> 00:19:44.640
as 17 days after first hearing news

00:19:44.640 --> 00:19:45.600
that their family members

00:19:45.600 --> 00:19:47.460
were killed by an accident in the mine,

00:19:47.460 --> 00:19:50.580
Maria Segovia and
everyone else in Camp Hope

00:19:50.580 --> 00:19:54.524
hear the news that they have
been all found alive and well.

00:19:54.524 --> 00:19:56.610
(people cheering)

00:19:56.610 --> 00:20:00.300
- Euphoria in Chile as
33 miners are found alive

00:20:00.300 --> 00:20:01.290
in a collapsed mine.

00:20:01.290 --> 00:20:04.650
- It took 17 days to find the 33 miners.

00:20:04.650 --> 00:20:06.540
- [Reporter] Trucks
carrying mining equipment

00:20:06.540 --> 00:20:09.123
are cheered like heroes in a parade.

00:20:10.360 --> 00:20:11.850
(upbeat music)

00:20:11.850 --> 00:20:15.000
- [Narrator] But this is where
the real work just begins.

00:20:15.000 --> 00:20:16.740
Now, that the workers have been found,

00:20:16.740 --> 00:20:18.810
it's time to make first contact with them

00:20:18.810 --> 00:20:21.633
and figure out how to
actually get them out.

00:20:23.550 --> 00:20:25.230
A camera and a makeshift phone

00:20:25.230 --> 00:20:27.150
is lowered down through the borehole,

00:20:27.150 --> 00:20:29.910
and the President of
Chile, Sebastian Pinera,

00:20:29.910 --> 00:20:31.920
is there to speak with Urzua

00:20:31.920 --> 00:20:33.270
who confirms once more

00:20:33.270 --> 00:20:35.703
that they are all alive and uninjured.

00:20:36.570 --> 00:20:40.560
The most important first step
is to get the man stabilized.

00:20:40.560 --> 00:20:42.750
Two additional boreholes are created

00:20:42.750 --> 00:20:45.900
to serve as a lifeline
to the trapped workers.

00:20:45.900 --> 00:20:49.710
The first initial hole is
used to send down supplies.

00:20:49.710 --> 00:20:52.500
The second hole is used for communication.

00:20:52.500 --> 00:20:55.140
And the third hole is
intended to be used for later

00:20:55.140 --> 00:20:59.550
as a potential point of
rescue to get the men out.

00:20:59.550 --> 00:21:01.080
And to the dismay of the miners,

00:21:01.080 --> 00:21:03.330
the first shipment of supplies isn't food,

00:21:03.330 --> 00:21:06.030
but rather bottles of glucose gel.

00:21:06.030 --> 00:21:09.180
Since they haven't had a
proper meal in 18 days,

00:21:09.180 --> 00:21:11.610
the miners can't jump
straight into real food,

00:21:11.610 --> 00:21:13.770
or it'll make them sick.

00:21:13.770 --> 00:21:16.470
They are happy to at least
receive fresh water though,

00:21:16.470 --> 00:21:17.730
meaning they no longer need

00:21:17.730 --> 00:21:20.790
to drink from the
industrial tank full of oil.

00:21:20.790 --> 00:21:23.850
The 33 miners also receive
a shipment of medicine

00:21:23.850 --> 00:21:27.060
containing vitamins and vaccinations.

00:21:27.060 --> 00:21:30.480
Urzua decides to appoint
fellow miner, Yonni Barrios,

00:21:30.480 --> 00:21:32.400
as their resident doctor in charge

00:21:32.400 --> 00:21:34.200
of delivering the injections,

00:21:34.200 --> 00:21:36.930
and Barrios will routinely
take man's vitals,

00:21:36.930 --> 00:21:38.310
such as blood pressure,

00:21:38.310 --> 00:21:42.380
in order to report back to the
medicine team above ground.

00:21:42.380 --> 00:21:43.950
(intense music)

00:21:43.950 --> 00:21:46.740
Meanwhile, Sougarret starts
coming up with a plan

00:21:46.740 --> 00:21:49.170
for how to get them out of the mine.

00:21:49.170 --> 00:21:51.690
All of the holes so far were narrow,

00:21:51.690 --> 00:21:54.510
but now they would need to
drill a much wider hole,

00:21:54.510 --> 00:21:57.930
which they estimate could
take up to four months,

00:21:57.930 --> 00:22:01.260
time in which the giant diorite
block could move further

00:22:01.260 --> 00:22:04.050
and potentially collapse the mine.

00:22:04.050 --> 00:22:05.520
To speed the process up,

00:22:05.520 --> 00:22:07.290
Sougarret decides to, once again,

00:22:07.290 --> 00:22:10.170
employ multiple strategies at once,

00:22:10.170 --> 00:22:12.960
and he comes up with three plans.

00:22:12.960 --> 00:22:15.930
Plan A is use a drilling
rig that is used in mines

00:22:15.930 --> 00:22:20.070
to create new shafts called a Strata 950.

00:22:20.070 --> 00:22:22.350
It will first need to drill a pilot hole

00:22:22.350 --> 00:22:23.610
to reach its target,

00:22:23.610 --> 00:22:26.250
then drill the same
hole to widen it enough

00:22:26.250 --> 00:22:28.143
that the man can be raised through.

00:22:28.980 --> 00:22:31.200
Plan B is to use an air core drill

00:22:31.200 --> 00:22:36.200
that typically drills wells
called a Shram 2130XD.

00:22:36.240 --> 00:22:39.630
This one will use one of the
free boreholes already created

00:22:39.630 --> 00:22:42.450
as its pilot hole and work to widen it,

00:22:42.450 --> 00:22:43.800
first to 12 inches,

00:22:43.800 --> 00:22:47.760
and then widen it once again to 28 inches.

00:22:47.760 --> 00:22:50.610
Plan C is to use a
powerful oil drilling rig

00:22:50.610 --> 00:22:54.090
called a RIG-421 that
won't need a pilot hole

00:22:54.090 --> 00:22:56.880
and can theoretically
dig the required width

00:22:56.880 --> 00:22:58.719
in just one single pass.

00:22:58.719 --> 00:23:00.180
(intense music)

00:23:00.180 --> 00:23:01.830
The entire operation would become known

00:23:01.830 --> 00:23:04.050
as Operation San Lorenzo,

00:23:04.050 --> 00:23:07.110
named after the patron saint of mining.

00:23:07.110 --> 00:23:09.210
While all three drills are busy digging,

00:23:09.210 --> 00:23:11.550
another team begins
working on the capsules

00:23:11.550 --> 00:23:13.020
that the workers will need to enter

00:23:13.020 --> 00:23:15.840
in order to be hoisted up to the surface.

00:23:15.840 --> 00:23:17.880
To achieve this, officials from NASA

00:23:17.880 --> 00:23:19.680
are called in to provide advice

00:23:19.680 --> 00:23:22.740
to the Chilean Navy for
constructing the capsule.

00:23:22.740 --> 00:23:23.640
And in total,

00:23:23.640 --> 00:23:27.345
three of these Phoenix rescue
capsules are manufactured.

00:23:27.345 --> 00:23:29.220
(intense music)

00:23:29.220 --> 00:23:32.670
Meanwhile, Plan C is
running into an issue.

00:23:32.670 --> 00:23:34.380
This is a drill that should be able

00:23:34.380 --> 00:23:37.290
to create the rescue
hole in just one pass.

00:23:37.290 --> 00:23:39.510
However, because of its massive size,

00:23:39.510 --> 00:23:40.920
the angle it has to drill,

00:23:40.920 --> 00:23:43.110
and because of the type of diorite rock

00:23:43.110 --> 00:23:44.160
it must chew through,

00:23:44.160 --> 00:23:46.710
it continuously wanders off target

00:23:46.710 --> 00:23:49.260
and needs to be stopped and recalibrated,

00:23:49.260 --> 00:23:51.425
significantly slowing its progress.

00:23:51.425 --> 00:23:52.890
(intense music continues)

00:23:52.890 --> 00:23:55.110
The next issue comes with Plan B.

00:23:55.110 --> 00:23:58.020
Despite a strong start with
significant progress made,

00:23:58.020 --> 00:24:00.180
reaching nearly half of the depth required

00:24:00.180 --> 00:24:02.310
in its first widening pass,

00:24:02.310 --> 00:24:04.980
the drill bit breaks
off into the borehole,

00:24:04.980 --> 00:24:07.260
effectively blocking further progress

00:24:07.260 --> 00:24:09.780
and making the hole useless.

00:24:09.780 --> 00:24:11.640
And to make matters even worse,

00:24:11.640 --> 00:24:14.820
Plan A runs into issues
with its hydraulics,

00:24:14.820 --> 00:24:17.943
causing its drilling
to also come to a halt.

00:24:18.900 --> 00:24:20.730
The sounds of the drill stopping

00:24:20.730 --> 00:24:24.270
sends panic and anxiety
through the men trapped below,

00:24:24.270 --> 00:24:25.950
as it becomes more realistic

00:24:25.950 --> 00:24:26.820
that their rescue

00:24:26.820 --> 00:24:29.943
really may not come for
several more months.

00:24:30.780 --> 00:24:34.080
Most of the miners' hopes
were pinned on Plan B,

00:24:34.080 --> 00:24:36.360
and so rather than
giving up on the borehole

00:24:36.360 --> 00:24:38.460
and starting all over again,

00:24:38.460 --> 00:24:42.030
the engineers came up
with a different idea.

00:24:42.030 --> 00:24:45.270
They devise a special metal contraption

00:24:45.270 --> 00:24:46.920
that is lowered into the hole

00:24:46.920 --> 00:24:50.580
and is able to retrieve the broken bit.

00:24:50.580 --> 00:24:52.585
And surprisingly, it works.

00:24:52.585 --> 00:24:53.820
(intense music continues)

00:24:53.820 --> 00:24:55.140
With the path cleared,

00:24:55.140 --> 00:24:57.990
drilling for Plan B can resume,

00:24:57.990 --> 00:25:00.840
news that come as a relief to everyone.

00:25:00.840 --> 00:25:02.190
(reporter speaking non-English language)

00:25:02.190 --> 00:25:03.390
- Today is the day.

00:25:03.390 --> 00:25:05.160
As we speak, rescue teams

00:25:05.160 --> 00:25:08.010
are continuing to test an empty capsule

00:25:08.010 --> 00:25:10.050
to make sure that it's
safe enough for the miners

00:25:10.050 --> 00:25:11.220
when they make the journey up.

00:25:11.220 --> 00:25:13.373
- They'll be told to keep their hands in

00:25:13.373 --> 00:25:16.447
and their faces back as that door closes.

00:25:16.447 --> 00:25:17.790
(drill rattling)

00:25:17.790 --> 00:25:20.100
- [Narrator] Finally, after 65 days

00:25:20.100 --> 00:25:21.630
of being trapped underground,

00:25:21.630 --> 00:25:25.890
what everyone has been so
eagerly waiting for happens.

00:25:25.890 --> 00:25:28.590
The Plan B drill finishes
widening its borehole

00:25:28.590 --> 00:25:30.330
enough that the team can lower

00:25:30.330 --> 00:25:32.010
the Phoenix capsules through,

00:25:32.010 --> 00:25:36.750
and the rescue operation
can enter its final phase.

00:25:36.750 --> 00:25:39.840
First, a rescuer is lowered
down into the borehole

00:25:39.840 --> 00:25:42.030
in order to assist the men from the bottom

00:25:42.030 --> 00:25:44.550
with instructions on
how to enter the capsule

00:25:44.550 --> 00:25:47.340
and make sure they are properly secured.

00:25:47.340 --> 00:25:49.290
And when this rescuer reaches the bottom

00:25:49.290 --> 00:25:50.790
and steps out of the capsule,

00:25:50.790 --> 00:25:53.730
the 33 workers finally
get their first sight

00:25:53.730 --> 00:25:57.571
of someone other than themselves,
someone from up above.

00:25:57.571 --> 00:25:59.760
(intense music)

00:25:59.760 --> 00:26:03.930
And now it's time to start
sending the men up one by one.

00:26:03.930 --> 00:26:05.790
The order in which the men will go up

00:26:05.790 --> 00:26:07.590
has already been determined.

00:26:07.590 --> 00:26:11.850
And first up is the shift's
foreman, Florencio Avalos.

00:26:11.850 --> 00:26:14.310
He and the three men who
will follow after him

00:26:14.310 --> 00:26:15.780
are deemed the most fit.

00:26:15.780 --> 00:26:17.190
And so if something goes wrong,

00:26:17.190 --> 00:26:18.780
they will be most likely to be able

00:26:18.780 --> 00:26:20.700
to extract themselves from the capsule

00:26:20.700 --> 00:26:22.765
and make their way back down the borehole.

00:26:22.765 --> 00:26:25.800
(intense music continues)

00:26:25.800 --> 00:26:29.010
As Avalos begins his
ascent up towards freedom,

00:26:29.010 --> 00:26:31.080
he begins to feel cool air

00:26:31.080 --> 00:26:33.206
the closer he gets to the surface.

00:26:33.206 --> 00:26:35.220
(intense music continues)

00:26:35.220 --> 00:26:38.130
The entire journey takes about 15 minutes,

00:26:38.130 --> 00:26:40.290
and as his head pokes above ground,

00:26:40.290 --> 00:26:43.620
he catches a glimpse of a
crowd of people in hardhats

00:26:43.620 --> 00:26:46.585
breaking out in cheers
of joy and celebration.

00:26:46.585 --> 00:26:48.660
(intense music continues)

00:26:48.660 --> 00:26:51.360
Up there, he is reunited with his family

00:26:51.360 --> 00:26:53.340
and greeted by the rescue crew,

00:26:53.340 --> 00:26:56.098
as well as by the Chilean President.

00:26:56.098 --> 00:26:58.710
(intense music continues)

00:26:58.710 --> 00:27:01.050
After Avalos is successfully extracted,

00:27:01.050 --> 00:27:03.420
the next three fit workers are brought up,

00:27:03.420 --> 00:27:06.120
followed by those deemed most at risk.

00:27:06.120 --> 00:27:08.340
- [Reporter] Joy for the rescued miners

00:27:08.340 --> 00:27:10.530
and pride for an entire country.

00:27:10.530 --> 00:27:12.960
- [Reporter] It's the
greatest underground rescue

00:27:12.960 --> 00:27:14.760
the world has ever seen.

00:27:14.760 --> 00:27:17.280
- [Narrator] Each cycle
is faster than predicted,

00:27:17.280 --> 00:27:19.350
and, though the rescue started at night,

00:27:19.350 --> 00:27:21.360
as the morning sun begins to rise,

00:27:21.360 --> 00:27:24.450
the remaining men are
instructed to wear sunglasses

00:27:24.450 --> 00:27:27.300
in order to protect their
eyes from the harsh sunlight

00:27:27.300 --> 00:27:30.762
after being underground
for over two months.

00:27:30.762 --> 00:27:31.680
(intense music fades out)

00:27:31.680 --> 00:27:35.100
Finally, it comes time for the
last worker to be raised up

00:27:35.100 --> 00:27:38.543
to the surface, Shift Supervisor Urzua.

00:27:38.543 --> 00:27:39.780
(intense music)

00:27:39.780 --> 00:27:43.020
And cheers continue to ring
out as he takes his turn

00:27:43.020 --> 00:27:45.129
to step outside of the capsule.

00:27:45.129 --> 00:27:48.360
(crowd cheering and applauding)

00:27:48.360 --> 00:27:51.930
Next, he declares to
President Pinera that,

00:27:51.930 --> 00:27:55.263
as the boss, he's now
handing the shift over.

00:27:56.550 --> 00:27:58.260
All that's left of the rescue now

00:27:58.260 --> 00:27:59.730
is to bring up the rescuers

00:27:59.730 --> 00:28:02.730
who were sent down to
aid in the operation,

00:28:02.730 --> 00:28:04.898
and to send the miners
off to the hospital.

00:28:04.898 --> 00:28:06.030
(siren wailing)

00:28:06.030 --> 00:28:07.080
All things considered,

00:28:07.080 --> 00:28:09.540
the workers are in great physical shape.

00:28:09.540 --> 00:28:12.900
All but two of the workers are
released almost immediately,

00:28:12.900 --> 00:28:15.870
with one man needing
further psychological tests

00:28:15.870 --> 00:28:18.900
and the other requiring some dental work.

00:28:18.900 --> 00:28:20.610
After only a few more days,

00:28:20.610 --> 00:28:22.950
the last two men have
been released as well,

00:28:22.950 --> 00:28:25.350
and all men are able to return home

00:28:25.350 --> 00:28:28.020
where in most cases they
would find themselves

00:28:28.020 --> 00:28:30.055
bombarded by journalists.

00:28:30.055 --> 00:28:32.280
(suspenseful music)

00:28:32.280 --> 00:28:34.290
- The rumors are proven to be true.

00:28:34.290 --> 00:28:37.230
There has been an agreement
between the 33 miners

00:28:37.230 --> 00:28:40.569
that there are some things that
could perhaps remain untold.

00:28:40.569 --> 00:28:41.700
(suspenseful music continues)

00:28:41.700 --> 00:28:43.440
- [Narrator] While they
were trapped in the mine,

00:28:43.440 --> 00:28:46.380
the 33 had decided to make a pact.

00:28:46.380 --> 00:28:47.550
Should they be rescued,

00:28:47.550 --> 00:28:49.950
they will stand together as a unit

00:28:49.950 --> 00:28:53.460
and only talk to one person
that they all agree upon

00:28:53.460 --> 00:28:55.440
as to how they survived.

00:28:55.440 --> 00:28:58.680
This way, no one man
would profit off the story

00:28:58.680 --> 00:29:00.570
any more than the others.

00:29:00.570 --> 00:29:04.110
However, because of this pact,
once the 33 were rescued,

00:29:04.110 --> 00:29:07.110
the universal support for
the men trapped in the mine

00:29:07.110 --> 00:29:09.120
began to crumble.

00:29:09.120 --> 00:29:10.950
They did not speak with the journalists

00:29:10.950 --> 00:29:13.470
that continuously showed up at their door,

00:29:13.470 --> 00:29:15.330
and they refused to answer questions

00:29:15.330 --> 00:29:17.280
about what exactly happened

00:29:17.280 --> 00:29:20.073
in those 17 days before they were found.

00:29:21.150 --> 00:29:23.160
Almost as if in retaliation,

00:29:23.160 --> 00:29:25.170
the media began to fill in the gaps

00:29:25.170 --> 00:29:27.840
with their own stories about the men,

00:29:27.840 --> 00:29:29.820
and none were as apparent as the one

00:29:29.820 --> 00:29:32.610
that spread like wildfire about the miner

00:29:32.610 --> 00:29:36.554
who once acted as the resident
doctor, Yonni Barrios.

00:29:36.554 --> 00:29:37.800
(somber music)

00:29:37.800 --> 00:29:40.290
The story ran that he had cheated

00:29:40.290 --> 00:29:42.630
and that his wife and
his secret girlfriend

00:29:42.630 --> 00:29:45.060
first learned about
each other at Camp Hope

00:29:45.060 --> 00:29:46.830
because of the accident.

00:29:46.830 --> 00:29:50.580
In reality, Chile, with a
very Catholic population,

00:29:50.580 --> 00:29:54.810
had only just legalized
divorce about five years prior.

00:29:54.810 --> 00:29:58.050
And because of how new the
idea of divorce still was,

00:29:58.050 --> 00:30:02.490
many couples still legally
remained married, just separated.

00:30:02.490 --> 00:30:05.730
Barrios's wife and girlfriend
had known about each other

00:30:05.730 --> 00:30:07.740
for long before the accident,

00:30:07.740 --> 00:30:11.220
and Barrios himself mainly
lived with his girlfriend.

00:30:11.220 --> 00:30:14.550
Yet, once journalists saw
that Barrios had a girlfriend

00:30:14.550 --> 00:30:16.950
and a wife waiting for him in Camp Hope,

00:30:16.950 --> 00:30:18.270
before looking any further

00:30:18.270 --> 00:30:21.990
they published a story
of the two timing miner.

00:30:21.990 --> 00:30:23.460
Many stories were also published

00:30:23.460 --> 00:30:25.830
that painted the workers as freeloaders,

00:30:25.830 --> 00:30:28.530
just looking for a get-rich-quick moment

00:30:28.530 --> 00:30:30.330
because of their ordeal.

00:30:30.330 --> 00:30:31.770
A wealthy businessman donated

00:30:31.770 --> 00:30:34.140
5 million Chilean Pesos to each man,

00:30:34.140 --> 00:30:36.600
equal to about 10,000 US Dollars.

00:30:36.600 --> 00:30:38.190
And some of the men traveled the world

00:30:38.190 --> 00:30:41.310
to appear on talk shows
and meet celebrities.

00:30:41.310 --> 00:30:42.450
Stories were published

00:30:42.450 --> 00:30:46.080
that the workers were racking
up gifts and expensive trips,

00:30:46.080 --> 00:30:49.380
and yet in reality, any
actual help or compensation

00:30:49.380 --> 00:30:52.800
for their ordeal was nowhere to be found,

00:30:52.800 --> 00:30:55.683
not even within the legal system of Chile.

00:30:56.580 --> 00:30:58.410
After a three year investigation,

00:30:58.410 --> 00:31:01.290
no charges were ever
brought against the owners

00:31:01.290 --> 00:31:02.471
of the San Jose mine.

00:31:02.471 --> 00:31:03.780
(somber music continues)

00:31:03.780 --> 00:31:06.180
The $10,000 they received dried up,

00:31:06.180 --> 00:31:08.520
and they needed to get back to work

00:31:08.520 --> 00:31:09.450
with some of the men

00:31:09.450 --> 00:31:11.520
even choosing to go back to mining

00:31:11.520 --> 00:31:13.353
because that is what they knew.

00:31:14.393 --> 00:31:16.228
(intense music)

00:31:16.228 --> 00:31:20.430
The story of the Chilean miners
is undoubtedly inspiring,

00:31:20.430 --> 00:31:25.350
but it is also a story about
how media attention works.

00:31:25.350 --> 00:31:28.440
If it weren't for Maria
Segovia creating noise,

00:31:28.440 --> 00:31:32.130
the world might have
never noticed this story.

00:31:32.130 --> 00:31:34.290
And as much as the world
cared about the men

00:31:34.290 --> 00:31:36.270
when they were trapped down below,

00:31:36.270 --> 00:31:37.230
once they were up,

00:31:37.230 --> 00:31:39.630
the happy ending story was completed,

00:31:39.630 --> 00:31:41.343
and these men were forgotten.

00:31:42.832 --> 00:31:44.670
(intense music)

00:31:44.670 --> 00:31:47.100
The Chilean miner rescue
is a powerful story

00:31:47.100 --> 00:31:48.780
of human perseverance,

00:31:48.780 --> 00:31:52.050
and perseverance can come in many forms.

00:31:52.050 --> 00:31:54.810
Sometimes it can begin
with a simple choice,

00:31:54.810 --> 00:31:56.820
the choice to risk everything

00:31:56.820 --> 00:31:58.950
for a chance at something better,

00:31:58.950 --> 00:32:02.808
such as in my video about the
incredible balloon escape.

00:32:02.808 --> 00:32:04.260
(intense music continues)

00:32:04.260 --> 00:32:06.570
Back when Germany was a divided country

00:32:06.570 --> 00:32:10.260
with a brutal border strip
that made it impossible

00:32:10.260 --> 00:32:12.930
to flee from the East German dictatorship,

00:32:12.930 --> 00:32:16.563
two families came up with an insane idea.

00:32:17.430 --> 00:32:21.510
They decided to build their
own homemade hot air balloon,

00:32:21.510 --> 00:32:24.928
and they would risk their
lives to try to fly to freedom.

00:32:24.928 --> 00:32:26.250
(intense music)

00:32:26.250 --> 00:32:28.320
It is a thrilling story,

00:32:28.320 --> 00:32:30.960
and I actually got to
interview Gunter Wetzel,

00:32:30.960 --> 00:32:33.033
one of the men who built the balloon.

00:32:33.930 --> 00:32:37.740
This documentary is part of
my Nebula original series

00:32:37.740 --> 00:32:39.480
